@!
#! shell-command

Execute a command using /bin/sh. Powwow waits until your shell finishes,
but you can put jobs in the background with & as usual. Semicolons,
escapes and braces need to be escaped if they are to be sent to the shell.
@beep
#beep

Ring your terminal's bell (like #print (*7)).
@cancel
#cancel [number]

Without an argument, all editing sessions are cancelled; otherwise, only
the given session is cancelled. The corresponding editor processes are
brutally killed.
@capture
#capture [[>]filename]

Capture all output from the main MUD connection and your typed commands
to a local file. Type #capture without argument to close the file.
If the filename starts with a '>', new text is appended to the end of the
file instead of overwriting it. See also #setvar buffer.
@clear
#clear

Clear the input line. Used by spawned programs before they print to the
screen when #option autoclear is off.
@cpu
#cpu

Show the CPU time used by powwow.
@do
@17
#do (expr) command
#n command

Repeat a command: #do evaluates the expression and uses the result as
counter, and #n uses a number, like #5 buy bread.

#do (3*8) north		(go north 24 times)
@edit
#edit

Show your active editing sessions, with a brief description and their
number.
@file
#file [=[definition-file]]

Set, show or clear the name of the definition file. By default it is the
one loaded when you start powwow, and powwow saves your settings to it
before exiting. To disable this autosave, use #file =
@group
#group [name] [on|off|list]

Turn on or off all the actions and aliases of a group, or list them.
To put an action in a group, write the group name after an '@' when
defining it:

#action >+auto-ride@non-pk ZBLAM!={#print;stand;ride}
#group non-pk off
@groupdelim
#groupdelim [delimiter]

Change the '@' between the label of an action or alias and its group name.
Without an argument, #groupdelim does nothing.
@help
#help [keys | math | command]

Without an argument, list all commands. '#help keys' shows the editing
keys, '#help math' the inline calculator, and '#help alias' or in general
'#help <command-name>' the help on a command. Names can be abbreviated.
@identify
#identify [startedit [endedit]]

Tell the server that powwow supports editing functions (currently only
MUME). It is best attached to an action triggered by a string in the
login screen. The startedit/endedit parameters are commands issued when an
editing session starts/ends.

#action >mume ***  MUME=#identify
@init
#init [=[command]]

Set up the command to execute on establishing a connection.

#init				(shows the command)
#init ={#identify;#speedwalk}	(sets the command)
#init =				(clears it)
@isprompt
#isprompt [number | (expression)]

Used inside a #prompt to declare that the beginning of the line is a prompt.
If positive, the number is the length of the prompt. If negative, -n, the
prompt is as long as the parameter $n. If 0 or missing, the whole line is a
prompt. See #prompt and the documentation for details.
@keyedit
#keyedit function

Run one of the line-editing functions, as if its key had been pressed.

#keyedit &prev-line
@load
#load [definition-file]

Set the definition file and load settings from it. Current settings are
completely erased first. To add the contents of a file to your current
settings, use #exe <filename instead.
@map
#map [-[number] | walksequence]

With no argument, show the map of the directions you travelled.

#map -1		(remove the last step from the map)
#map -		(clear the whole map)
#map nsssue	(add the list of directions to the map)
@movie
#movie [filename]

Like #capture, but add timestamps to each line, to allow replay at correct
speed with powwow-movieplay. powwow-movie2ascii converts a movie to plain
text. Type #movie without argument to close the file.
@net
#net

Show the amount of data transmitted to and received from the remote host.
@nice
#nice [{number | (expression)} [command]]

Set or show the place in the list for new actions and marks. When it is 0
(the default), powwow puts them at the bottom. With a command after the
number, the new value is used only for that command.

#nice 4 #mark Grizzly=red on blue	(put the mark in the 4th place)
@prefix
#prefix [string]

Begin each new line you type with the string. You can edit or delete the
inserted string as usual. #prefix without arguments removes it.

#prefix tell arthur
@prompt
#prompt [[<|=|>|%][+|-]label] [{pattern | (expression)}=[command]]

Like #action, but for prompts: powwow matches #prompts on the beginning of
a line, and the command must run #isprompt to recognize it as a prompt.
It works only on the main MUD connection, and is needed only to mess with
prompts in strange ways. See the documentation for details.
@qui
#qui

Do nothing. It exists so that #qui is not an abbreviation of #quit.
@quit
#quit

Exit from powwow. It brutally closes all connections that are still open,
without renting your characters.
@quote
#quote [on | off]

Toggle verbatim mode, in which no alias expansion takes place and
semicolons, escapes, braces and ` are sent as typed, unless the line begins
with a #. Useful to paste a text from an editor.
@rawprint
#rawprint {text | (expression)}

Like #print, but does NOT add a final newline, and can handle ASCII 0
characters.
@rawsend
#rawsend {text | (expression)}

Like #send, but does NOT add a final newline nor IAC escaping of ASCII 255
characters, and can handle ASCII 0 characters.
@rebind
#rebind name [sequence]

Change the control sequence of a key binding, but not its name or effect.
Without a sequence, you are asked to press the key.

#rebind f1 ^[OP		(tell powwow that your f1 key sends ESC O P)
@rebindall
@rebindALL
#rebindall
#rebindALL

#rebindall runs #rebind on most key bindings (skipping trivial ones like
^A, ^B, etc.), asking you to press each key. #rebindALL does the same for
really every key binding.
@record
#record [filename]

Record all text typed from the keyboard to a local file. Output from the
remote host is not recorded. Type #record without argument to close the
file.
@request
#request [editor][prompt][all]

Send identification strings to the server: editor is the same as #identify,
and prompt asks for the special sequence IAC GA at the end of each prompt,
which helps to detect it.
@retrace
#retrace [number]

Walk in the reverse direction of the last number of steps. If number is 0
or left blank, walk all the way back to where automapping started.
@save
#save [definition-file]

Set the definition file and save settings to it. Powwow also saves them
automatically before exiting. The definition file is overwritten only if
writing succeeds.
@setvar
#setvar name[={number|(expr)}]

Set or show an internal variable. Known variables are:

buffer	bytes of recent text from the MUD to keep and write at the beginning
	of #capture and #movie files when you open them (default 0)
lines	the number of lines of your terminal
mem	the maximum length of a text or string, in bytes (default 0: no limit)
timer	the number of milliseconds since program start

#setvar timer=0			(reset internal timer to 0)
#setvar timer			(show timer and let you edit it)
@snoop
#snoop session-id

Toggle the output display of a session.
@spawn
#spawn session-id command

Create a new session connected to a shell command instead of a MUD.
Writing to ##<session-id> sends data to the command's standard input, and
its standard output is executed as if typed from the keyboard. Each command
it outputs must end with a newline or a semicolon, and the last one with a
newline. See #option autoclear and #clear for printing to the screen.
@speedwalk
#speedwalk [walksequence]

Execute a speedwalk sequence even if the speedwalk option is disabled.
@time
#time

Show the current time and date. Useful if you want to use #at.
@ver
#ver

Show the current version, some compile options and when powwow was compiled.
@zap
#zap session-id

Disconnect a session.
